A-S143 / S143 — one original slow-client journey budget

Open learning\S143\index.html directly in a browser.
Training rehearsal - synthetic data - not a live Azure result

All profiles, clients, reconnects, delays, attempts, results, versions, clocks,
deadlines, budgets and state records are fixed fictional teaching DATA ONLY.
No network, clock, traffic, load-generator, live client or user-data operation
occurs. Buttons reveal records synchronously, not after actual delays. There
is no service, server, account, credential, storage or arbitrary data entry.

EXACT SOURCES — read before behavior design
S143: Slow clients and network variability: Validate latency and connection
recovery under explicitly supported network conditions.
D-S143: Add bounded slow-client/network variability through labeled
client-driver conditions.
Acceptance: Original journey budgets include retries/reconnects; emulated
conditions and observed native effects are distinguished.
Original dependency: D-S042 (forced termination and over-budget retries count
as interruptions; this portable lesson does not complete that source).
A-S143: Slow a client/reconnect; retries must remain inside the original
journey budget. SOURCE gate; P1; SC-S143; frozen original Kano S.
B-S143: Conduct one labeled network-variability profile; verify real client
impact and emulation limits. CORE, DATA, HOST, C090 gates remain unmet.
evidence\raw\source\source-contract.json preserves the exact original rows,
inventory card, source commit, raw CRLF and independent canonical Git hashes.
It is not OP150/source150/catalog availability or a live acceptance snapshot.

CONDUCT / INSPECT / MEASURE
1. Choose one closed preset; Start fixes journey-amber/client-amber on
   fixture-v1: original start 1000 ms, budget 3000 ms, deadline 4000 ms.
2. Record slow interruption: first client wait 800 + first service 700 =
   1500 ms, observed at 2500. attempt-1 is interrupted and retained.
3. Record backoff/reconnect: normally backoff 400 + reconnect 300 = 700 ms;
   cumulative 2200, observation 3200, remaining upper bound 800.
   fixture-v2 is supplied version DATA, not proof of actual migration.
4. Inspect final attempt: normally final wait 200 + service 500 = 700 ms;
   original cumulative 2900, completion 3900, remaining 100.
   Inspecting complete-looking DATA does not certify PASS.
5. Measure original budget: all six independent checks must PASS. Equality
   at the original deadline passes; 4001 fails. HTTP 200 or a short new
   attempt cannot reset the original start/deadline or hide cumulative time.
6. Reset controls / retain history: resets current controls and error,
   but accepted/rejected records, original timing, outcomes and interruption
   traces remain. A reset-before-measure is UNKNOWN unless a known failure
   is already present. Reload intentionally discards this in-memory DATA.

PRESETS AND EXACT THRESHOLDS
within-budget: elapsed 2900, completion 3900, remaining 100, PASS.
exact-deadline: final service 600; elapsed 3000, completion 4000,
remaining 0, PASS. Original deadline equality is inclusive.
one-ms-over: final service 601; final attempt 801 is below 3000 but
elapsed 3001, completion 4001, remaining -1, FAIL despite HTTP 200.
per-attempt-reset: final service 1300; final attempt 1500 is below 3000
but elapsed 3700, completion 4700, remaining -700, FAIL.
missing-final-time: HTTP 200, completion=true and correct=true DATA supplied;
final wait/service/observation/completion time/elapsed are null, UNKNOWN.
missing-completion: HTTP 200 and final observation 4000 supplied; completion
and correctness are null. Completion time/elapsed remain null, UNKNOWN.
Observation time is not invented as completion time.
late-unknown: backoff 1101 + reconnect 600 after the first 1500 = known
cumulative 3201, observation 4201, remaining upper bound -201. Final
completion/time/elapsed remain null; known budget failure remains FAIL.
reconnect-failed: known failed recovery; no final attempt/completion/time
is invented; FAIL dominates remaining unknown observations.
incorrect-200: correct original result=false despite elapsed 2900/HTTP 200,
FAIL. Connection and response success do not establish correctness.
unsupported-condition: an explicitly outside-support condition despite
good timing/result DATA, FAIL. This is a labeled fixture, not an emulator.

OUTPUT SHAPES / UNCERTAINTY
Pure CommonJS/browser API: createState(), execute(state, command),
inspect(state). Returned states, presets and outputs are recursively frozen.
State keys: schemaVersion, phase, runCount, current, journal, history,
outcomes, rejections. Phases: idle, started, slowed, reconnected, observed,
measured. Original keys: journeyId, clientId, initialVersion, startMs,
budgetMs, deadlineMs. The original never changes within a run.
Current retains run, presetId, original, profile, attempts, recovery, final,
knownElapsedMs, interruptions, verdict, records. Every attempt/wait/service/
backoff/reconnect remains inspectable, including in archived outcome.trace.
Cumulative keys: clientWaitKnownMs, serviceKnownMs, backoffKnownMs,
reconnectKnownMs, knownElapsedMs, completedAtMs, elapsedMs,
remainingAtKnownObservationMs, remainingAtCompletionMs, finalAttemptMs,
perAttemptWithinBudget, unknownFinalTiming.
Unknown is null, never zero, true, a guessed final clock or a new deadline.
Known component sum is a lower bound when final time is unknown; remaining
at that known observation is an upper bound, not a promised remaining SLA.
The final-attempt-within-budget flag is diagnostic only, never a vote.
Checks: supported-condition, connection-recovery, original-deadline,
final-response, final-completion, correct-result. Known FAIL wins over
UNKNOWN; only all six PASS plus explicit Measure certify lesson PASS.
An expected negative may report preset verifier MATCH while user impact is
interrupted. Passing recovery reports recovered-after-interruption, retains
attempt-1's interruption and does not rewrite earlier failing journeys.
Before any supplied interruption, initial or early-reset impact is unknown;
no nonexistent earlier interruption is invented. model-02 retains the genuine
regression assertion failure, followed by the resource-free IaC N/A refactor.
Labeled condition evidence: fictional-emulation-data-only.
Observed native effect: unverified — not observed. Native effects are never
inferred from fixture timing, HTTP 200, response correctness or version DATA.
Declared teaching support is one fictional client and at most two supplied
attempts: first wait 800, known final wait 200; backoff 400 or 1101; reconnect
300 or 600; known final service 500/600/601/1300 ms. These closed DATA labels
are not a claim about native support. Missing final timing remains unobserved.

STRICT / BOUNDED / ACCESSIBLE
12 runs, 72 accepted actions and first 12 rejected actions per page.
Exact commands: {type:'start',presetId:<closed string>} or
{type:<slow|reconnect|observe|measure|reset>}. No coercion, extra fields,
getters, symbols, sparse/extra arrays, cycles, nonplain prototypes, functions,
arbitrary timing or result overrides. Rejected arbitrary input is replaced by
a fixed invalid-action marker, not retained as user text.
Every supplied state must exactly match a bounded canonical command replay;
forged origin, budget, time, version, final completion, result, history,
rejection or archived outcome fails ERR_STATE without trusting that result.
Native buttons/select, semantic labels, visible focus and role=status/alert
support keyboard conduct. Success focuses the next permitted action; rejection
focuses the error. Reset focuses the select or exhausted-bound status.
HTMLFormElement.prototype.reset.call(form) avoids form.reset shadowing.
Missing/invalid local model safe-holds with disabled conducting controls and
UNKNOWN, not a browser exception or a certified outcome. Rendering uses
textContent/DOM creation; CSP permits only self assets and exact theme/style
hashes, denies inline handlers, arbitrary scripts, connections and form actions.
No general network sandbox or full accessibility certification is claimed.

BDD → LOADABLE STUB → GENUINE RED → IaC N/A → IMPLEMENT / REFACTOR → GREEN
original-budget.feature preceded the loadable stub. Actual isolated Edge
keyboard Start stayed idle without page errors. The genuine model assertion
failed ERR_ASSERTION ('idle' !== 'started'), child exit 1. The immutable
stub/spec/source byte snapshots and full raw stdout/stderr are retained in
evidence\raw\red-01\ with non-test raw suffixes outside autodiscovery.
evidence\iac-na.json records explicit IaC N/A AFTER RED and BEFORE
implementation. No cloud, IaC, scaffold, identity, credential, deployment,
spend, package, framework, download or unsafe browser flag was used.
The initial explicit producer capture lacked a uniform exact-environment
guard. It is retained as historical provenance, not relabeled as opt-in proof.
evidence\raw\qualify-01\ deliberately replays the byte-identical archived stub
and genuine assertion under exact CAPTURE_BROWSER_EVIDENCE=1 after that guard
was tightened. This is post-implementation requalification, not a replacement
for the original pre-implementation RED chronology, and earns zero credit.

From the repository root (smallest complete focused set):
  node --test --test-concurrency=1 learning\S143\model.test.cjs learning\S143\integrity.test.cjs learning\S143\browser.test.cjs
  node --check learning\S143\model.js
  node --check learning\S143\app.js
  node learning\S143\record.cjs verify
The verify command also asserts every owned source/evidence byte unchanged.
Normal verification is READ-ONLY. Only exact CAPTURE_BROWSER_EVIDENCE=1
deliberately creates a new unique exclusive raw browser observation and source
snapshot. Unset, 0, true and 01 never capture. All current producer
red/qualify/model/syntax/green record commands also require exact opt-in and
unique phase-NN names; they are not the normal verification path.
Raw failures and prior captures are never overwritten or included as tests.
Narrow raw-byte attributes preceded capture. Raw CRLF/byte hashes and
canonical LF/Git blob identity are independently preserved and checked;
legitimate LF Git checkout is not a raw-evidence rewrite.

